WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.880
Hello, so recently i've gone through the
grades of people like Einstein and

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:08.160
Nikola Tesla
and on those videos some of you asked

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:12.639
that I show some of my own grades
and I thought well today as a bit of a

00:00:12.639 --> 00:00:18.000
special video to celebrate
passing 500k subscribers that I would do

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.400
just that. So I'm going to show you
through the transcript I have here

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:26.160
of my time at the University of
Canterbury which is where I did my

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:29.439
undergraduate degree and it was a
Bachelor of Science

00:00:29.439 --> 00:00:34.399
majoring in physics and maths. I'll put
the university grading scale up on the

00:00:34.399 --> 00:00:37.120
screen
so you can have a little look it goes

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:41.200
from an A+
which means you got a mark of between 90

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:45.600
and 100 for the course
all the way down to an E and i think

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:50.800
anything above
a D would be considered a passing grade.

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:54.079
There are then a couple of special
grades such as P

00:00:54.079 --> 00:00:58.960
and F which are usually for things that
aren't worth credit or are considered

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:03.920
differently to an actual course.
So let's start with 2013 which was my

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:08.799
first year of uni,
and the year is broken up into two main

00:01:08.799 --> 00:01:12.720
semesters
and to be considered a full-time student

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:16.720
you would take
four courses in each semester, each

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:21.680
course is worth 15 points
so if you keep that up over three years

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:24.960
you would have the 360 points that you
need

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:28.880
to graduate. Now i'll highlight the
courses that i took

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:32.720
during my first semester which is when I
was 17.

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:38.400
I got two A+ an A and
an A- now this was probably one of

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:42.720
my best semesters
at uni because i was fresh out of high

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:45.920
school, i was motivated i was pretty hard
working

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:49.520
and i was fairly organized so much so
that i

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:52.560
do remember actually reading and
studying

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:56.479
some of the lecture materials before
showing up to the lecture

00:01:56.479 --> 00:02:00.079
which is a good practice but
unfortunately not something that i was

00:02:00.079 --> 00:02:04.719
able to keep up for very long.
I took courses in math, physics

00:02:04.719 --> 00:02:08.720
astrophysics
and computer science. Now of those the

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:12.560
computer science course was definitely
the most difficult for me

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:16.720
and i think that's because i had never
encountered computer science before.

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:21.360
This class was my first introduction to
any of the concepts of coding.

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:26.160
So first semester was fine but second
semester would go on to probably be my

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:31.120
worst semester of my degree
it was one where i felt stressed a lot

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:34.000
of the time,
i was disorganized, i would show up to

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:37.840
lectures late.
I had this one class that i really hated

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:42.080
which was math 170
and maybe i hated it because i didn't

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:46.560
really have any friends
in that class or i don't know why but i

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:49.280
remember
when the exam time came around i just

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:52.800
felt like i couldn't focus. I couldn't
focus on studying

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:56.239
i showed up to the exam and asked
someone as we were waiting outside the

00:02:56.239 --> 00:02:59.200
door to be let in, to explain Bayes
Theorem to me.

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:03.040
You know that's how last minute i was
kind of panicking about this

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:08.319
whole semester. At the same time i was
taking part in this university business

00:03:08.319 --> 00:03:12.239
competition
and trying to launch a startup and i was

00:03:12.239 --> 00:03:17.200
also working on some other projects
to do with video interviews and

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:21.840
things for the physics department.
A couple of notes is that this academic

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:24.480
writing assessment wasn't a course it
was just

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:28.400
a little test we had to do to prove we
were proficient at

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:34.959
writing and this astro
211, the two indicates that it was a

00:03:34.959 --> 00:03:39.040
second year course whereas all of the
others would be considered first-year

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:42.560
courses so that's kind of reflecting
their difficulty.

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:46.879
So let's move on to my second year and
you may notice here that i've taken

00:03:46.879 --> 00:03:52.080
10 courses instead of the usual eight
and there's no real reason why i've done

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:55.120
that and in fact i ended up getting a
lot more

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:58.959
points and i needed to graduate so you
wouldn't

00:03:58.959 --> 00:04:04.080
do this as a strategic option i guess
but the extra courses that i added were

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:09.200
ones that i really wanted to take
and so i felt prepared to do extra work

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:11.760
in both semesters to be able to take
them.

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:15.280
I did all the standard courses in maths
and physics

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:18.479
and then i added this science
communication

00:04:18.479 --> 00:04:22.880
third year course which i guess kind of
makes sense to what i do now, i was

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:27.600
interested in science communication back
then and i also added on this

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:31.440
first year computer science course which
was a follow-up to the one i'd taken in

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:34.560
first year
and i kid you not i decided to take that

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:37.840
course
just because i had a dream about it.

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:41.600
I had a dream about
one day before the deadline to sign up

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:45.199
for courses
that i had taken this course and that,

00:04:45.199 --> 00:04:48.240
I don't know, there was just this feeling
that it was the right thing to do

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:52.800
so i woke up and i enrolled in it but i
actually was really glad about that

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:56.160
decision.
It was a course all about sort of

00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:01.120
algorithms and data structures
and in the internship that i did at the

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:04.720
end of this second year
it was in an observatory and we were

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:09.520
processing images of galaxies,
the skills that i learned in that class

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:12.400
were pretty much the most useful thing
i'd learned

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:17.199
all year in terms of you know how to
process these images using code.

00:05:17.199 --> 00:05:20.479
The grades this year were pretty good i
got quite a few A+

00:05:20.479 --> 00:05:25.680
and no B's which i was happy
about. Of these courses the hardest one

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:29.759
was probably Analysis and Groups MATH240.

00:05:29.759 --> 00:05:33.919
but the one that required the most work
was probably PHYS285

00:05:33.919 --> 00:05:37.039
Experimental Physics which i managed to
get an A+ in

00:05:37.039 --> 00:05:42.400
but it took a lot of work because it was
a lab course where every week we would

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:47.840
do like a three hour experiment
and then every week have to write up a

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:51.840
long lab report and send that in. It
would be about 10 or so

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:55.199
pages with all the code and everything
and

00:05:55.199 --> 00:05:59.199
yeah i guess a few of the weeks of this
course i did have to pull an all-nighter

00:05:59.199 --> 00:06:03.360
to get that lab report done
but yeah i managed to do well in it in

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:09.280
the end. Probably my favorite course this
year was MATH203 linear algebra

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:13.680
which i also got an A+ in and i
don't know it was just a concept i

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:18.080
enjoyed, i thought the lectures were
nice everything sort of fit together and

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:23.360
made sense in a way and
yeah it was an area of math that

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:27.440
i felt like i was good at.
During 2014 i did still have a lot of

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:31.919
stuff going on
outside class, i was the vice president

00:06:31.919 --> 00:06:35.840
of the physics society that year and
still working on i guess lots of little

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:41.520
science communication projects
but i think compared to the the previous

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:46.319
semester things were a lot more calm.
I was in general less stressed and a bit

00:06:46.319 --> 00:06:50.800
more organized.
My third and final year i again made the

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:55.199
odd choice
to take ten courses instead of eight

00:06:55.199 --> 00:06:59.199
and this time i did all of the usual
courses

00:06:59.199 --> 00:07:06.479
in maths and physics and then i added on
this PSYCH106 course which i was able

00:07:06.479 --> 00:07:09.360
to take under this program the uni had
called the Vice

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:12.960
Chancellor's Award where people were
able to take

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:20.160
a course outside of their usual program,
for me that would be outside of physics,

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:23.840
and take it without having to pay for it
and they would only be marked either

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:27.039
pass or fail
so it wouldn't really affect their gpa

00:07:27.039 --> 00:07:31.599
and they would be able to have
a try of something a little bit new. So i

00:07:31.599 --> 00:07:35.680
took this psychology course
and yeah i kind of enjoyed it. My hardest

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:40.560
course this semester was MATH302
partial differential equations which

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:44.560
i got another B
in which again i wasn't happy about but

00:07:44.560 --> 00:07:47.120
um
yeah that course i don't know i just

00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:51.599
found it really hard.
The mid-term test of this course i

00:07:51.599 --> 00:07:55.120
actually got 50% on. 
After getting that really bad

00:07:55.120 --> 00:07:58.000
midterm result
i was like man i'm going to do terribly

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:01.840
in this course i should
withdraw from it because i've got way

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:04.479
more than enough points that i need to
graduate

00:08:04.479 --> 00:08:10.000
i don't even need to take it. But i went
to the admin of the university and they

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:13.919
said i was one day too late to be able
to withdraw from the course so i just

00:08:13.919 --> 00:08:16.800
had to stay in it
and i guess i'm glad i did there would

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:21.440
have been no real benefit to me
pulling out of things even if i'm going

00:08:21.440 --> 00:08:24.800
to get a low grade which might look bad
on my transcript

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:28.240
well at the time i didn't think i'd be
showing it to thousands of people on the

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:31.680
internet
but i guess it was better to just keep

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:35.360
it and learn the things i did learn in
that course than trying to run away from it.

00:08:35.360 --> 00:08:38.880
That year i also did dynamical systems

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:42.800
which i really liked
it's one of my favorite courses but

00:08:42.800 --> 00:08:46.399
definitely the course i loved the most
